ndard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ of the system, we get\n![](images/11/11e0da79656b7f8e2c84c6c54e6dd6b54cf53cbb8e746d44dfb13329c3d2ed8f.svg)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n**Note:** Recall that for the general solution of the ordinary differential equation $x'(t)=ax(t),$ we have \n\n$$\nx'(t)=ax(t)\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\nx(t) = c \\, e^{at}, \n\\quad\nc \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nNow, for the general solution of the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} \\, \\mathbf{c},\n\\quad\n\\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.\n$$\n\nLet's see more examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230188 #edb/ref 1065 14515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230188 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "978fc2f4-910c-5bef-9ae3-3da9377642ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230188 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230192 #edb/ref 1064 "Before proceeding with the next example, let's recall some important properties of matrix exponentials:\n\n1\\. *Law of Exponents:* If $A$ is a constant square matrix, then\n\n  $$\n  e^{A(t+s)} = e^{At}\\,e^{As}.\n  $$\n\n2\\. *Inverse:* For any $t,$ the matrix $e^{At}$ is invertible and \n\n  $$\n  (e^{At})^{-1} = e^{-At}.\n  $$\n\n3\\. *Identity:* For any square matrix $A,$\n\n  $$\n  e^{A\\cdot 0} = I.\n  $$\n\n4\\. *Product of exponentials:* If $A$ and $B$ commute (i.e., $AB = BA$), then\n\n  $$\n  e^{A}e^{B} = e^{A+B}.\n  $$\n\n  **Watch out!** In general, this identity is *not true* unless $A$ and $B$ commute.\n\nLet's see how these properties can be used in practice." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230192 #edb/ref 1065 14528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230192 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5ae3cfa7-a5a5-5a50-aefa-8b8dec561567" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230192 #edb/ref 1067 "Reminder: Properties of Matrix Exponentials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230193 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, let's find the solution to the initial value problem below\n![](images/31/3111f065d441881e050417b345b34947509f175f00ebd5402963e432d013348e.svg)\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/5c/5c4ed0a2e9db0979e4fc0342c2e4db7c23c60cf58293a496fa661ff1c03fccde.svg)\nwhere ![](images/71/7164659247c5290544655b05943d7d998f67cb1cc3b4cc45bab0902e8059bc75.svg) So, the matrix of the system is ![](images/e3/e3f94e8ce2cc2063d469fbac2af87232cc130f788243c1507bfe9972dd4b04c1.svg)\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\, \\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is some constant vector.\n\nEvaluating both sides of $\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} \\mathbf{c}$ at $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={Φ}_{0}(0)\\,c \\\\ x(0) & ={e}^{A(0)}\\,c \\\\ x(0) & =I\\,c \\\\ c & =x(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to our initial value problem is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\, \\mathbf{x}(0).\n$$\n\nComputing the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ of the system, we get\n![](images/fc/fc4e1ba16be420fbe5e53e2dbb4ee6da96c7097bbe4e117315407ec4dc65365f.svg)\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{7t} & t{e}^{7t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(3-2t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -2{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230193 #edb/ref 1065 14529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230193 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9cb11887-9535-5a54-a180-eb20a7534696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230193 #edb/ref 1067 "Solving Initial Value Problems" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230194 #edb/ref 1064 "Next, let's find the solution to the initial value problem \n![](images/93/93f9c58b8a9cea64ba97f43f2831336ea8d38a6fd6165211a54d2822415c1497.svg)\ngiven that ![](images/ee/ee25228bf71d1b7d9d0688af06a7f1335c252310127b403ed0ed37f7fe277c07.svg) where ![](images/3a/3a3ac9326741c0e2390a8e2f079808f0b432b740149ef4b034ffa7568dc33848.svg)\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/2b/2b81cf358ffb3df49f327b63138640ac452a249f989353b521be34d531aeffd9.svg)\nwhere ![](images/11/1110f0bdbe784b21683bfb9610ca9c04e5a45755954d49a15124e530feef2002.svg) So, the matrix of the system is ![](images/47/47d8a0214e5ccdf6625a330d23003dec11cbc3a4f35cb2992df29a688963e4b2.svg)\n\nGiven the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution is $\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} \\, \\mathbf{c}.$ We can determine the constant vector $\\mathbf{c}$ using the initial condition at time $t=t_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t_0) = e^{At_0} \\, \\mathbf{c} \\implies \\mathbf{c} = e^{-At_0} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0),\n$$\n\nsince the inverse of $e^{At_0}$ is $e^{-At_0}.$ Plugging this back into our general solution gives the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our specific problem (with $t_0=1$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7(t-1)) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7(t-1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7(t-1)) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230194 #edb/ref 1065 14530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230194 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0fe9a975-9d92-5f79-8a67-34af55196fb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230194 #edb/ref 1067 "Solving Initial Value Problems With Shifted Conditions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305859 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305859 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305859 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "488cb1f7-d8f3-5937-97b2-5b2c3a66bfcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305962 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305962 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305962 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5392cdab-374c-5ae2-97a2-f4228e352881" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306320 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306320 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306320 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7a6f36b6-4121-5cab-9216-833c7c62083f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306341 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306341 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306341 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7cea238c-07f8-5863-ba42-90a07